  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-141235\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is <em>always</em> a polynomial. </p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Polynomials are <em>not</em> closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is <em>not</em> always a polynomial. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction. </p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Expression III is <em>not</em> a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n<mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" display=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"8\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math display=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"margin-left: 0px; margin-right: 0px;\"><mjx-mtable style=\"min-width: 9.688em;\"><mjx-table><mjx-itable><mjx-mtr><mjx-mtd style=\"text-align: right; padding-right: 0px; padding-bottom: 0.4em;\"><mjx-mstyle><mjx-mfrac><mjx-frac type=\"d\"><mjx-num><mjx-nstrut type=\"d\"></mjx-nstrut><mjx-mrow><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2212\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c33\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-msup><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c33\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup></mjx-mrow></mjx-num><mjx-dbox